# Untitled

Mixed-Valence $\mathbf{Cu^{I}/C u^{I I I}}$ Metal–Organic Frameworks with Noninnocent Ligand for Multielectron Transfer
## Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are one of the most fascinating classes of extended solids. Their modular design and synthesis (i.e., by varying metal sources, organic linkers, and synthetic conditions) allow the structures and properties of MOFs to be fine-tuned; as a result, desired functionalities, porosities as well as chemical and thermal stabilities may be customized for specific applications.[1] Due to their ability to utilize the redox reactions at both the metal and organic moieties, redox-active MOFs have attracted more attention for applications in energy storage, electrocatalysis, molecular electronics, gas separations and storage, electrochromic devices, and microporous conductors.[2] Transition metal copper in the unusual trivalent oxidation state has been of chemical and theoretical interest to the scientific community because of its role as a reactive intermediate in several chemical, biological, and catalytic reactions and as a constituent of materials with interesting conductive and magnetic properties.[3] Known for high reactivity and instability, up until now, formally $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I I)}$ species have primarily been evidenced in a limited number of discrete metal complexes,[4] where the electronic structures and oxidation state assignment remain an intriguing topic of interest.[5]
Mixed valency has been shown as an important mechanism for charge transfer and delocalization resulting in electronic conductivity.[6] When two or more electroactive moieties exist in more than one formal oxidation state, electronic coupling will occur and result in rapid oscillation of charge between the sites (i.e., a process called intervalence charge transfer).[6a] It is known that conductivities correlate strongly with the extent of mixed valency due to the controlled carrier concentration.[7] Incorporating mixed valency via redox-active metal ions and organic ligands into porous MOFs has great promise to allow increased framework delocalization and conductive extended solid-state systems.[6a] Redox-active organic bridging ligands capable of facilitating through-bond charge transfer may further induce long-range delocalization. Strong interactions between the metal sites and organic ligands permit the formation of continuous energy bands with delocalized charge carriers.[7]
Ligands which possess highest occupied molecular orbitals (HOMOs) close in energy to the lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals (LUMOs) of the metal ion can best allow electronic communication between the metal nodes. Wellmatched energy levels and good orbital overlap will, therefore, lead to small band gaps and high charge mobilities which are required for conductivity.[7] Currently, intrinsically mixed-valence MOFs are still relatively rare due to a prerequisite that their metals and/or redox-active ligands must synergistically facilitate redox isomerism.[6a]
Herein, we report two novel three-dimensional (3D) mixed-valence copper-benzoquinoid MOFs $[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{3}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ and $\left[\mathbf{Cu}_{4}\mathbf{L}_{3}\cdot\mathbf{Cu}(\mathrm{iq})_{3}\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ $(\mathrm{LH}_{4}=1,4$ -dicyano-2,3,5,6-tetrahydroxybenzene,[8] $\mathrm{iq}=$ isoquinoline), denoted as NCKU-52 and $\mathrm{NCKU-}52\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu(iq)}_{3}$ , respectively $\mathbf{\DeltaNCKU=N}$ ational Cheng Kung University).[9] The structural and chemical properties of the compounds have been thoroughly elucidated by various spectroscopic characterization techniques and computational studies. To the best of our knowledge, the formally $\mathrm{Cu(I)/Cu(III)}$ mixed valency has, for the first time, been revealed in MOFs. The existence of formally $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I I)}$ species in metal–organic extended solid-state structures is also unprecedented. The redox-non-innocent ligand L, which provides chelating functional groups conjugated with the aromatic core, is utilized as a bridging unit, allowing through-bond charge transfer between the Cu metal sites. Therefore, long-range delocalization may be realized in the extended 3D structures.[7] The metalbased mixed valency coupled with the non-innocent ligand L allows NCKU-52 to exhibit semiconducting behavior with an electronic conductivity in the order of $10^{-8}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ .[10] These MOFs with the three-dimensionally connected channels have been investigated for the electrochemical properties, unveiling ionic transport to/from the redox-active sites at both the copper and organic ligand L. The insight gained from this work is valuable for the rational design of 3D MOFs with intrinsic electronic conductivity and multielectron redox activity.
## Results and Discussion
To design 3D MOF-based materials capable of efficient charge transfer, both metal and organic ligands which can form continuous chains of coordination bonds must be taken into consideration.[2b,11] A majority of MOFs rely on organic linkers which usually form relatively ionic bonds with the metals (e.g., carboxylate or imidazolate linkers), thus leading to weak charge delocalization between the redox-active metal centers.[2a,7] Conventional 3D MOFs mostly show low electronic conductivity $(<10^{-10}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1})$ , caused by the large separation between metal ions by multiple atoms and insulating organic ligands with poor orbital overlap with the metals. Balancing the porosity and intrinsic electronic conductivity of 3D MOFs, therefore, remains a challenging task. In order to promote better interactions between the sites, it is necessary to build strong covalent bonding and direct conjugation between the metal and organic linker for long-range conduction pathways.[7,12] To tackle this, we utilized a redox-active non-innocent ligand $\mathrm{LH}_{4}$ to synthesize the 3D MOFs, which allow d- $\pi$ conjugation and stronger delocalization between the mixed-valence metals and organic redox centers, therefore facilitating charge transport and providing electronic conductivity.[13]
To synthesize NCKU-52, using anthracene as template, $\mathrm{LH}_{4}$ was reacted with $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $N,N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $11\mathrm{d}$ to afford brownishblack single crystals (Figure S1). The single crystal X-ray analysis reveals a 3D network with an empirical chemical formula $[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{3}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (Figure 1a). The Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectrum of NCKU-52 shows the stretching band at $1641~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ attributed to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ of the benzoquinoid ligand, whereas $\mathrm{LH}_{4}$ does not show carbonyl signal (Figure S2). This reveals that when deprotonation and oxidation occur during the synthesis, the two adjacent hydroxyl groups on $\mathrm{LH}_{4}$ become $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ and $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O}^{-}$ , giving a total of two negative charges. Two types of coordination geometries of the $\mathtt{C u}$ metal centers ratio 1 :3 are found: (1) octahedral $\mathrm{cu}$ center (green polyhedron in Figure 1a) coordinated by three pairs of the oxygen atoms from three ligands L, and (2) tetrahedral $\mathrm{cu}$ center (blue polyhedron in Figure 1a) coordinated by one pair of the oxygen atoms from ligand and two nitrogen atoms from the cyano groups of two additional ligands. For charge balance, the formal oxidation states of the two $\mathtt{C u}$ centers can be assigned as $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I I)}$ (octahedral) and $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ (tetrahedral), and thus the chemical formula of NCKU-52 is $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu(III)Cu(I)_{3}L_{3}]_{n}}$ . The pore size of this MOF is ${\approx}7\mathrm{\AA}$ determined by measuring the distance between the centroids of parallel ligands (Figure 1a). These pores are aligned as 1D channels (Figure 1b), which interconnect with one another three-dimensionally (Figure 1c). The as-synthesized NCKU-52 was transferred into fresh DMF and soaked for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1d}$ . The crystals were filtered and washed with DMF and ethyl acetate, followed by drying in air, to afford NCKU-52-DMF. The pore size of $\mathrm{\Delta}\bar{7}\mathrm{\AA}$ in NCKU-52 is about twice the distance of $\pi$ -stacking, which inspired us to manipulate its space by inserting molecules available for $\pi\cdot\pi$ interactions. Isoquinoline was added into the mixture of $\mathrm{LH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMF solution, followed by heating at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{d}$ to afford brownish-black single crystals of NCKU-5 $\mathsf{\Omega}_{2\mathrm{-Cu(iq)}_{3}}$ (Figure S1). The single crystal $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray analysis reveals a 3D framework which is the same as that of the parent compound NCKU-52, but with additional $\mathrm{{Cu}(i q)_{3}}$ complex partitioning its pores via $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking (Figure 1d). The planar fused rings of the coordinating isoquinoline align in parallel with the benzene rings of ligand L in all three directions (Figure 1e and 1f). The ${\bf N}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Cu-N}}$ angle of $\mathrm{{Cu}(i q)_{3}}$ complex is $113.8^{\circ}$ (Table S4), which is close to the bond angle of a tetrahedral complex $109.5^{\circ}$ . However, the single crystal $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray analysis shows insufficient electron density around the Cu, preventing assignment of counteranion to compensate the charge of $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ with iq being a neutral ligand. It is worth noting that the constraints of the framework geometry may play a role in the shape of the $\mathrm{{Cu}(i q)_{3}}$ complex. Electron delocalization to ligand iq ( $\overset{\cdot}{\pi}$ -acceptor) and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between the complex and the framework with the non-innocent ligand L may also complicate the valence assignment of the metal in $\mathrm{{Cu(iq)}}_{3}$ complex.[14]
The phase purity of these MOFs is confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis, showing good agreement between the experimental and simulated patterns (Figure 2a, S3, and S4). The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) reveals that NCKU-52 is thermally stable until $280^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 2b).
The pores of NCKU-52 can be filled with ${\approx}25~\mathrm{wt}\%$ of DMF molecules as suggested by the TGA trace of NCKU-52- DMF (Figure S5). For NCKU-52- $\mathrm{{Cu}(i q)_{3}}$ , $\approx34\%$ weight loss corresponds to the removal of $\mathrm{{Cu(iq)}}_{3}$ complex, followed by the decomposition of main framework at ${\approx}470^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S5). This indicates that NCKU- $52\mathrm{-Cu(iq)_{3}}$ is thermally and structurally more stable than the other two materials. ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurement of NCKU-52 at $77\mathrm{K}$ was performed after the activation of the sample by supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(\mathsf{s c-C O}_{2})$ approach.[15] The Brunauer–Emmett– Teller (BET) surface area of NCKU-52 is $572\pm15\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and the calculated pore size is $6.0\mathring\mathrm{A}$ (Figure 2c). The PXRD patterns of NCKU-52 after $\mathbf{sc-CO}_{2}$ activation and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption remain consistent, indicating the structural stability of the activated MOF (Figure 2a). The electrical conductivities of these MOFs were measured by four-point probe method (Figure 2d and S6), revealing the semiconducting feature of NCKU-52 $(1.0{\times}10^{-8}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1},$ , and the insulating properties of NCKU-52-DMF $(4.9{\times}10^{-10}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{NCKU-}52\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu(iq)}_{3}$ $(9.6{\times}10^{-11}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1})$ . It is worth noting that although ligand L, with oxygen atoms coordinating to the metals, may not be a relatively soft, electropositive linker, the fact that it can allow charge delocalization into the aromatic core can lead to better metal-ligand orbital overlap and energy matching, giving rise to semiconducting behavior in NCKU-52.[7] Surprisingly, in spite of the more compact and stable packing of $\mathrm{NCKU-}52\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu(iq)}_{3}$ , the additional $\pi-\pi$ interactions introduced by $\mathrm{{Cu(iq)}}_{3}$ seem to disturb the long-range electron conduction pathways along the d- $\pi$ conjugation of the 3D framework.[11b,16] Overall, redox-active NCKU-52 provides moderate electrical conductivity and three-dimensionally interconnected voids, potentially allowing facile electron and ion transport which have barely been observed in 3D MOFs.
High-resolution Cu K-edge X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) experiments were performed on the single crystals of NCKU-52 and NCKU-52-DMF at Japan Synchrotron Radiation Research Institute (JASRI) SPring-8 in order to elucidate the local electronic valence of the $\mathrm{cu}$ ions in the compounds. Figure 3a shows the high-resolution $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) spectra of the two MOFs. Interestingly, both the NCKU-52 and NCKU-52- DMF show pre-edge features occurring at $8981.4\mathrm{eV}$ which falls within the range of $\mathrm{8981\pm0.5\mathrm{eV}}$ for $1\mathrm{s}{\rightarrow}3\mathrm{d}$ transition previously reported for $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I I)}$ complexes.[3a,17] This preedge maximum shifts $+1.4\mathrm{eV}$ with respect to that of the $\mathrm{cu}$ foil $(\approx8980~\mathrm{eV})$ .[3a] The overall Cu K-edge features of the two compounds resemble those of the coupled binuclear $\mathrm{Cu(I)/Cu(III)}$ complex reported elsewhere, with no characteristic features of $\operatorname{Cu}(0)$ and $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ .[17a]
The comparison between NCKU-52 and NCKU-52-Cu$(\mathrm{iq})_{3}$ was also carried out by using Cu K-edge XAS at National Synchrotron Radiation Research Center (NSRRC), Taiwan (Figure 3b and 3c). The XANES spectra of these two mixed-valence MOFs are quite similar, with a pre-edge positioned at $8981.2\mathrm{eV}$ for NCKU- $52\mathrm{-Cu(iq)}_{3}$ which is slightly shifted to a lower energy compared to that of NCKU-52. The pre-edge energy has been used to correlate with oxidation state for a number of transition metals, such as V, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, and Cu, due to its separation from the complicated structure of the rising edge and the principal edge maximum.[3a] The second derivative spectra are used to clearly determine the peak energies (Figure 3c).[3a,17a] It is obvious that both NCKU-52 and $\mathrm{NCKU-}52\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu(iq)}_{3}$ have very different edge features from those of $\mathtt{C u O}$ ; therefore, the presence of $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ in the samples may be ruled out. The rising edge of NCKU-52- $\mathrm{{Cu}(i q)_{3}}$ which is slightly lower in energy compared to that of NCKU-52 together with some edge features resembling $\mathrm{cu}$ foil suggests the existence of oxidation state lower than $^{1+}$ from the entrapped $\mathrm{{Cu}(i q)_{3}}$ complex. It is noteworthy that in the $1{\mathrm{s}}{\to}4{\mathrm{p}}$ energy region which is between the pre-edge and principal maximum,[3a] the two compounds do not show well-resolved peaks in the XANES spectra; however, the second derivative of the edge data reveals visible peaks particularly with inflection points at ${\approx}8987.5{\scriptstyle-}8989.5\ \mathrm{eV}.$ which are not observed in any of the $\operatorname{Cu}(0)$ , $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ , and $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ reference compounds in the same region. This may imply the presence of a higher oxidation state of $\mathtt{C u}$ species.[3a] It is worth noting that unambiguously assigning the physical oxidation states remains a challenge due to the complexity of the materials under study. The long-range order in the solid-state materials and the lack of basic information on the $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I I)}$ K-edge with a small number of well-characterized compounds as reference further complicate the characterization.[3a,5a] It is known that in addition to the metal oxidation state, metal K-edge structure varies with the nature of the ligands (coordination number, type, and geometric arrangement).[3a,17b] Covalency of the ligand also influences the energy positions, and ligand field splitting has been reported to increase or decrease the energy of $_{\mathrm{1s\to3d}}$ transition.[3a,5a] It has been revealed that the metal-ligand bonding of formally high-valent $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{III})$ species is extremely covalent which, as a result, can lead to Cu K-edge absorption features with energies that complicate physical oxidation state assignment.[5a,17b] Previous evidence suggests that formally $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I I)}$ complexes tend to have significantly diminished Cu d-character in their LUMOs with predominantly ligand-orbital character (also known as ligand field inversion).[5a,18] Moreover, $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{III})}}$ species is found to have limited capacity to be oxidized, which necessitates localization of electron hole character on the supporting ligands.[3a,5a] This increase in metal-ligand covalency has been shown to decrease band gaps and result in increased conductivities/charge mobilities.[7] Considering all these aspects mentioned above, the two different coordination geometries of $\mathtt{C u}$ centers linked together especially by the non-innocent ligand L (believed to be a strong $\upsigma$ -donor capable of stabilizing a high-valent $\mathrm{cu}$ species) will greatly complicate the metal-ligand bonding situation in our formally $\mathrm{Cu(I)/Cu(III)}$ MOFs.[3b]
Although the detailed mechanism of Cu redox reaction leading to the formation of the mixed-valence MOFs is not well understood, previous studies indicate the importance of non-innocent ligand L used as an organic building unit.[19] The atomic charges of NCKU-52, calculated using the Bader partitioning Scheme,[20] show that all tetrahedral $\mathrm{cu}$ centers carry a $+0.93$ charge, which is essentially consistent with the expected $^{1+}$ oxidation state. The formally $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{III})}}$ octahedral centers exhibit more positive $+1.20$ charge, because of the charge redistribution from the non-innocent ligand $\mathrm{~L~}$ in the NCKU-52 MOF. This is also consistent with the calculated electrostatic potential map (Figure 3d), where negative regions shown in red reside primarily on the ligand’s donor groups. In the case of $\mathrm{{Cu(iq)}}_{3}$ complex in NCKU- ${\cdot}52\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}C u(i q)}}_{3}$ , the Cu center coordinated to the iq ligands carries a $+0.68$ charge, somewhat less positive than the tetrahedral $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ center in the framework. This observation may be attributed to the distinct charge redistribution depending on the organization of the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacked framework and the local environment around Cu in the crystal structures.
Thus, while the existence of $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I I)}$ is still debatable, it might be more appropriate to say that the higher oxidation state of $\mathtt{C u}$ in the synthesized MOFs has only a “formal” oxidation state $^{3+}$ , better described as physically $\mathrm{{d}^{10}\ C u(I)}$ , when it is bound by three ligands L.[17a] However, it is worth noting that introduction of formally mixed-valence Cu sites seems to affect the valence band structure and density of states (DOS) near the Fermi edge of NCKU-52. Indeed, the DOS (Figure S7) calculated using density functional theory (DFT) is characteristic of a semiconductor with a band gap of $2.34\mathrm{eV}$ , and the Fermi level is primarily dominated by electronic contribution from the Cu centers.[7,21] Thus, unlike many other 3D MOFs, NCKU-52 can be more conductive through an electron transport mechanism enabled by the mixed-valence Cu sites.[6c] The DOS calculation results are in full agreement with the experimentally measured electrical conductivity, confirming the semiconducting behavior of NCKU-52.
The magnetic characteristics of the mixed-valence MOFs, NCKU-52 and $\mathrm{NCKU-}52\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu(iq)}_{3}$ , were analyzed using continuous-wave electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy at X-band frequency. Figures 3e and 3f show normalized temperature-dependent spectra of the two compounds when the temperature is decreased from 298 to $120\mathrm{K}$ . It is worth noting that $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ $(3\mathrm{d}^{10})$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{III})~(3\mathrm{d}^{8})$ , both in physical states, do not contribute to the X-band EPR spectra (EPR silence).[22] However, it is obvious from the spectra that as the temperature goes down, the signals become substantially stronger, suggesting the presence of $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}$ $(3\mathrm{d}^{9})$ ground state, with an electron spin $S{=}1/2$ , interacting with the nuclear spin $I^{\mathrm{Cu}}=3/2$ of the $^{63,65}\mathrm{Cu}$ isotopes.[6d,23] Figure S8 shows the experimental and simulated X-band EPR spectra of NCKU-52 and NCKU-52- $\mathrm{{Cu(iq)}_{3}}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ . For NCKU-52, the parameters used for simulations in the spin Hamiltonian are $g_{||}=2.383$ and $g_{\perp}=$ 2.090, $\mathbf{A}_{|\cdot|}=126\mathbf{G}$ and $\mathbf{A}_{\perp}=20\mathbf{G}$ $(33\%)$ ; $g_{\mid\mid}=2.370$ and $g_{\perp}=2.100$ , $\mathbf{A}_{|\cdot|}=148\mathbf{G}$ and $\mathbf{A}_{\perp}=40\mathbf{G}$ $(67\%)$ . NCKU-52- $\mathrm{{Cu(iq)}}_{3}$ yielded comparable data, with $56\%$ of the former component and $44\%$ of the latter. It is important to note that the XAS data, collected at $298\mathrm{K}$ , provide insights into the oxidation state of the $\mathrm{cu}$ ions, unequivocally ruling out the existence of $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ at room temperature. This apparent discrepancy between the EPR results and the oxidation states inferred from the other $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray spectroscopic techniques implies that valence tautomerism may be induced as a function of temperature. This interesting phenomenon has also been reported on Cu complexes with non-innocent behaviors.[22]
Based on the results above, the redox activity of the doubly-mixed-valence MOFs, NCKU-52, NCKU-52-DMF, and $\mathsf{N C K U-}52\mathrm{-Cu(iq)}_{3}$ , is worth exploring. Benzoquinoid ligand $\mathrm{~L~}$ is known to reversibly undergo a $2\mathrm{e}^{-}$ transfer reaction.[8] If all the $\mathtt{C u}$ metal centers are reduced to $\mathtt{C u(0)}$ , NCKU-52 $\mathrm{[Cu(III)Cu(I)_{3}L_{3}]_{n}}$ can undergo $12\mathrm{e}^{-}$ transfer per formula unit, corresponding to a high theoretical capacity of $393\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ . The two electron-withdrawing nitrile groups can also help enhance charge delocalization, which gives rise to a relatively high redox potential, ideal for a cathode material. The three isoreticular MOFs under study have different pore-filling environments: (1) NCKU-52 with three-dimensionally interconnected $7\mathrm{\AA}$ channels (activated); (2) NCKU-52-DMF with pores filled with DMF molecules; (3) $\mathsf{N C K U-}52\mathrm{-Cu(iq)}_{3}$ with pores partitioned by $\mathrm{{Cu}(i q)_{3}}$ complex via $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions.
The MOFs were investigated as cathode material in Li cells. The multiple voltage plateaus observed in the voltage profile in Figure S11 are in agreement with the cyclic voltammogram (CV) (Figure 4a), which suggests multielectron redox reactions of NCKU-52 therefore resulting in a high specific capacity (one electron transfer corresponds to $32.8\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}},$ . At a current density of $200\mathrm{\mAg^{-1}}$ (Figure 4b), NCKU-52 delivers a discharge capacity of $338\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ corresponding to ${\approx}10\mathrm{e}^{-}$ transfer (after the deduction of capacity contribution from the conductive carbon) within the voltage range of $1.5{-}3.9\mathrm{V}$ . Surprisingly, at a rate as high as $12000\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ , a capacity of $252\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ still can be obtained. The electrochemical properties observed for this compound are superior to those from most 3D-MOF-based electrode materials reported previously (Table S5). These results suggest the redox activities at both the metal and the organic redox centers for NCKU-52 as well as the other two MOFs (as shown in Figure S10–S13). Galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) measurements (Figure S14) suggest that NCKU-52 has better kinetics with smaller overpotential compared with the other two compounds. Regardless of their lower electronic conductivities, apparently, the pathways for Li-ion transport in these two materials could be limited because the pores of NCKU-52-DMF are filled with disordered DMF molecules, and the space of $\mathrm{NCKU-}52\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu(iq)}_{3}$ is partitioned by $\mathrm{{Cu}(i q)_{3}}$ complex. Interestingly, as observed in the capacity retention plots (Figure S13), $\mathrm{NCKU-}52\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu(iq)}_{3}$ with $\pi-\pi$ stacked complexes in the pores shows higher reversible capacities than the other two compounds at the same cycling rates, which indicates its superior structural stability due to the additional $\pi-\pi$ interactions in 3D.
To reveal the change at the redox centers of the copperbenzoquinoid MOFs during discharge-charge processes, Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed on the NCKU-52-DMF and $\mathrm{NCKU-}52\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu(iq)}_{3}$ electrodes. The mechanistic study of NCKU-52 was not performed due to its relatively lower structural stability at low cycling rates. However, its redox reactions during cell cycling are believed to be similar to those of NCKU-52-DMF. As shown in Figure S18, starting with pristine electrode, the absence of Cu $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ satellite peaks at ${\approx}942\ \mathrm{eV}$ suggests that $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ species do not exist in the structure,[24] consistent with the XAS data. Cu $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}~2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ observed at 931.8 and $951.8\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ , while those at 933.7 and $953.8\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to the higher oxidation state of $\mathrm{cu}$ .[8,24]
It is worth noting that these positive shifts in binding energies from those of $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ imply the presence of a higher valence of $\mathtt{C u}$ species in NCKU-52-DMF, which is not $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ due to the absence of shakeup satellite peaks of $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ mentioned above.[3b] This may be an indicator of the trivalent oxidation state of the $\mathtt{C u}$ ions. It has been shown that the degree of the shifts of binding energies varies depending on the supporting ligands and the covalency nature of the metal-ligand bonds in the structure.[3b]
The deconvolution of the high-resolution $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathrm{p}$ spectra of discharged electrodes (Figure S18) shows gradual reduction of $\mathrm{cu}$ species to $\operatorname{Cu}(0)$ , at 933.0 and $953.0\mathrm{eV}$ ,[25] resulting in the majority being $\operatorname{Cu}(0)$ . On charge up to $3.9\mathrm{V}$ , some $\mathrm{cu}$ species are oxidized, and more $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ and the higher valence of $\mathrm{cu}$ are observed, while the majority still remains $\mathtt{C u(0)}$ . The XPS spectra of $\mathrm{{NCKU-}}52\mathrm{{-Cu(iq)_{3}}}$ exhibit a similar trend of $\mathrm{cu}$ redox activity except for the presence of additional $\mathrm{cu}$ with somewhat lower partial charge in pristine electrode, as discussed above (Figure S19). These results reveal that $\mathtt{C u}$ species are reduced and oxidized during discharge and charge process, respectively. The deconvolution of the C 1s XPS spectra also provides insight into the redox activities of the organic ligand L (Figure S20 and S21). The peaks attributed to C C, C O, and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ bonds[8,26] suggest that on discharge, the carbonyl is converted to enolate group while on charge, the reverse process takes place. This confirms the reversibility of the redox reaction of the organic moieties, in good agreement with our previous work on copper-benzoquinoid coordination polymers.[8,27]
To closely monitor the valence state change of Cu during cell cycling, in situ Cu K-edge XAS was carried out on NCKU-52-DMF electrode. Figure 5 shows in situ XANES data during first and second discharge-charge cycles. The signal of pristine electrode (Figure 5a) is consistent with that of NCKU-52-DMF crystals (Figure 3b). On discharge to $1.5\mathrm{V}$ (Figure 5a), the pre-edge peak at $8981.4\mathrm{eV}$ slowly shifts to a lower energy suggesting the reduction of $\mathrm{cu}$ species with the edge features characteristic of $\operatorname{Cu}(0)$ . On charge to $3.9\mathrm{V}$ (Figure 5b), a reverse process takes place with the curves shifting back to a higher energy close to the original position of pristine electrode. This increased photon energy at the end of charge indicates a greater effective nuclear charge on the more highly oxidized Cu species, suggesting the oxidation of $\mathrm{Cu.}^{[3\mathrm{a}]}$ The XANES data of the second cycle display a similar trend (Figure 5c and 5d), confirming the redox activity of the Cu species.
## Conclusion
We have synthesized novel 3D copper-benzoquinoid MOFs, NCKU-52 and NCKU- $52\mathrm{-Cu(iq)}_{3}$ , with unprecedented formally $\mathrm{{Cu(I)/Cu(III)}}$ mixed valency. The chemical and physical nature of these bench stable $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{III})}}$ -containing compounds have been thoroughly investigated by various spectroscopic techniques and computational studies. Highresolution Cu K-edge XAS accompanied by XPS provides insight into the local electronic valence and confirms the metal-based mixed valency. This is believed to be the first time that formally $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I I)}$ species are witnessed in extended metal–organic solids. The non-innocent ligand L used in conjunction with the $\mathrm{Cu(I)/Cu(III)}$ mixed valency has been shown to allow better metal-ligand orbital overlap of the d- $\pi$ conjugation, leading to strong long-range delocalization in the 3D framework. This results in a small band gap of $2.34\mathrm{eV}$ for NCKU-52 and an electronic conductivity characteristic of a semiconductor. The temperature-dependent EPR analysis reveals the valence tautomerism of the two materials. The oxidation state of the $\mathrm{cu}$ in $\mathrm{{Cu}(i q)_{3}}$ complex is found to be rather difficult to be conclusively assigned due to the sensitivity and limitations of the characterization techniques. Both XPS and XAS may suggest the presence of a valence lower than $^{1+}$ for this $\mathrm{cu}$ center which has a different environment from the tetrahedral $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ center in the framework. The ligand environment ( $\scriptstyle{\pi-\pi}$ interactions between the complex and the framework with non-innocent ligand L) may support electron density delocalization from the metal to ligand iq which is a $\pi$ -acceptor. Based on these data, it may be more appropriate to claim that the lower oxidation state of $\mathrm{cu}$ in $\mathrm{NCKU-}52\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu(iq)}_{3}$ is physically ${\tt d}^{10}$ $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ . These isoreticular MOFs with different pore environments, NCKU-52, NCKU-52-DMF, and $\mathrm{{NCKU-}}52\mathrm{{-Cu(iq)_{3}}}$ , have been demonstrated to undergo redox reactions with Li at both the $\mathrm{cu}$ metal and organic ligand L, showing high capacity and high rate capability as cathode for lithium-ion batteries. The results shown here underline the importance of mixed valency, non-innocent ligand, and pore environments on the electronic/ionic transport.
